#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
qscan - functional quality scan of one or more Git repositories, built on the
codebase-memory-mcp (CBM) knowledge graph.

Per repository it reports:
  1. entry points with registration evidence and a confidence level
     (HTTP/UI routes, GraphQL, gRPC, message consumers, schedulers/batch jobs,
     serverless functions, gateway routes, CLIs, library API, program roots)
  2. business flows: in-repo call chains plus calls that leave the component
     (HTTP clients, producers, gateway routes), linked to the entry points they reach
  3. expected functional scenarios derived from code evidence, each with file:line
  4. existing functional tests (code tests, Gherkin/BDD, Karate, Robot, Postman,
     JMeter, .http, load tests, Pact) at test-case level, mapped to entry points
  5. gaps with severity, confidence and evidence
  6. detected technologies, parser coverage and limitations
Several repositories passed together are treated as one system (cross-repo flows).

Design: a framework-agnostic core plus plugins (one class per framework/tool;
see PLUGINS). Each plugin runs isolated: a failure is reported, the scan goes on.
Deterministic: no LLM, no network. Same commit + rules -> same output.

Requirements: Python 3.9+, git, `codebase-memory-mcp` on PATH.

Usage:
    python qscan.py <repo> [<repo> ...] [--json out.json] [--coverage jacoco.xml]
                    [--public-root pkg/__init__.py] [--rules my_rules.json]
                    [--no-index] [--limit 50] [--fail-on high-gaps] [-v]
    python qscan.py --list-plugins | --dump-rules

Exit codes: 0 ok, 2 usage error, 3 CBM unavailable/failed, 4 indexing failed,
5 internal error, 6 quality gate failed (--fail-on).
"""
